um and De humani corporis fabrica helped establish a new kind of “visual argument” enabling scientific study of nature in sixteenth-century European medicine.","confidence":0.72},"academicOrientation":{"score":65,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.74,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Conrad Gessner","confidence":0.4}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"65d441f314abfab8001a002a68d2ae1513fcb0e76f323574e24554f2914d5f28","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Science","secondarySubjects":["Medicine & Public Health","History"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores human anatomy and medical botany through a scientific lens, with significant discussion relevant to both medicine and historical contexts.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"08069668d9927c4606fc0f0deaf3f963dee41dfeec742a9deb7c2ad010a73cb6","summary":"Because of their spectacular, naturalistic pictures of plants and the human body, Leonhart Fuchs’s De historia stirpium and Andreas Vesalius’s De humani corporis fabrica are landmark publications in the history of the printed book. But as Picturing the Book of Nature makes clear, they do more than bear witness to the development of book publishing during the Renaissance and to the prominence attained by the fields of medical botany and anatomy in European medicine. Sachiko Kusukawa examines these texts, as well as Conrad Gessner’s unpublished Historia plantarum, and demonstrates how their illustrations were integral to the emergence of a new type of argument during this period—a visual argument for the scientific study of nature. To set the stage, Kusukawa begins with a survey of the technical, financial, artistic, and political conditions that governed the production of printed books during the Renaissance. It was during the first half of the sixteenth century that learned authors began using images in their research and writing, but because the technology was so new, there was a great deal of variety of thought—and often disagreement—about exactly what images could do: how they should be used, what degree of authority should be attributed to them, which graphic elements were bearers of that authority, and what sorts of truths images could and did encode.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Art","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-picturing-the-book-of-nature-image-text-and-argument-in-sixteenth-century-human-anatomy-and-medi"}],"displaySummary":"Because of their spectacular, naturalistic pictures of plants and the human body, Leonhart Fuchs’s De historia stirpium and Andreas Vesalius’s De humani corporis fabrica are landmark publications in the history of the printed book. But as Picturing the Book of Nature makes clear, they do more than bear witness to the development of book publishing during the Renaissance and to the prominence attained by the fields of medical botany and anatomy in European medicine. Sachiko Kusukawa examines these texts, as well as Conrad Gessner’s unpublished Historia plantarum, and demonstrates how their illustrations were integral to the emergence of a new type of argument during this period—a visual argument for the scientific study of nature. To set the stage, Kusukawa begins with a survey of the technical, financial, artistic, and political conditions that governed the production of printed books during the Renaissance. It was during the first half of the sixteenth century that learned authors began using images in their research and writing, but because the technology was so new, there was a great deal of variety of thought—and often disagreement—about exactly what images could do: how they should be used, what degree of authority should be attributed to them, which graphic elements were bearers of that authority, and what sorts of truths images could and did encode.","primaryTopic":